   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to maternal mortality\nreporting and advancement of best practices; and in relation to providing\ninformation on possible complications from pregnancy",
    "basePrintNo": "S7622",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7622-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 20,
       "fullName": "Jesse Hamilton",
       "imgName": "889_jesse_hamilton.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 889,
       "sessionMemberId": 937,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "HAMILTON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §2509, amd §§2803-j & 2803-n, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7622\n\nSPONSOR: ALCANTARA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to maternal mortality\nreporting and advancement of best practices; and in relation to provid-\ning information on possible complications from pregnancy\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure that the Department of Health is taking steps to lower the\nState's maternal mortality rate by mandating more transparent reporting\nof maternal deaths, developing evidence-based procedures that maternal\ncare providers should adopt to prevent maternal mortality, and by ensur-\ning that pregnant women and new mothers receive information on compli-\ncations that could lead to death.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the public health law to create a new\nsection 2509. This section mandates that the department of health work\nwith experts and stakeholders to develop evidence-based procedures to\nlower maternal mortality and then work to ensure the adoption of those\nprocedures by providers. The Department will have to issue annual\nreports on maternal mortality in the state, the procedures created to\nlower maternal mortality, the rate of adoption of these new procedures,\nand the effect the procedures have had to lower maternal mortality. The\nstate shall also now provide data on maternal mortality disaggregated by\nrace or ethnicity and county.\n\nSection 2 amends § 2803-j of the public health law to mandate that leaf-\nlets provided to maternity patients include information about serious\ncomplications that could lead to death.\n\nSection 3 amends § 2803-n to ensure that the parental information\ninclude information on screenings for conditions identified to present\nserious threats to the health of mothers in the first year after preg-\nnancy.\n\nSection 4 establishes an effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSince the year 2000, the maternal mortality rate in the United States as\na whole has increased from a rate of 19 deaths per every 100,000 births\nto 24 per every 100,000 births. In New York State, the rate according to\nthe Department of Health was 25 deaths per 100,000 births in 2015 and in\nNew York City the rate was an alarming 31 deaths per 100,000 births.\nBoth of these rates were an increase from the rate the state and city\nexperienced in 2006. The sad fact that maternal mortality rates in the\nUnited States and New York have been on the increase while the rest of\nthe world as a whole continues to see declines is shameful.\n\nIn addition, the scourge of maternal mortality does not affect all women\nthe same. African American women suffer from maternal mortality at much\nhigher rates that women on other races in this country, and yet health\nauthorities have not collected the necessary data to explain and correct\nthis horrible disparity.\nThe reality is that New York can take steps to change this shameful\ntrajectory. The state of California has been able to avoid this national\ntrend and in fact has seen its maternal mortality rate decline signif-\nicantly thanks to the California Maternal Quality Care Collaborative,\nwhich has created a number of new treatment procedures that providers in\nCalifornia have started to use and that have had a demonstrably positive\neffect in lowering maternal mortality. New York should follow this path\nand take active steps to improve maternal care in ways that will save\nwomen's lives. The Department of Health should lead the way and work\nwith experts to create procedures to lower our maternal mortality rate.\nThe department also needs to ensure that we track the disparities in\nmaternal mortality between different populations in the State and make\nthese disparities public, to ensure public awareness and build the data\nsets that researchers will need to discover solutions.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nFiscal implications to be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect 90 days after it has become a law. Effective\nimmediately, the addition, amendment, and/or repeal of any rule or regu-\nlation necessary for the implementation of this act on its effective\ndate are authorized to be made and completed on or before such effective\ndate.\n",
